Titre$ Mise à jour de la clé des espèces européennes du genre Hypoxylon par J.Fournier, B.Köpcke et M.Stadler, New species of Hypoxylon from western Europe and Ethiopia
Com$ Mycotaxon 113, 2010 /  Adaptation par François Valade Dec. 2010
1,0$ Mature stromata carbonaceous, black, without KOH-extractable pigments [but immature stromata orange, with Dark Purple (80) to Dark Vinaceous (82) pigments]. Ascospores 9.5-11.5 × 4-5.5 µm.(USA, France) $ H. submonticulosum Y.M. Ju & J.D. Rogers
1,2$ Mature stromata waxy to woody, not carbonaceous, colored other than black and with KOH-extractable pigments
2,3$ Stromata hemispherical to almost spherical
2,6$ Stromata effused to pulvinate
3,0$ Stromatal surface Vinaceous Grey (116), Sepia (63) or Grayish Sepia (106), KOH-extractable pigments Pure Yellow (14), Greenish Yellow (16) or Citrine (13), ascospores 17-22 × 9-11 µm; on Fraxinus $ H. fraxinophilum Pouzar
3,4$ Stromatal surface Rust (39), Bay (6), or Dark Brick (60), KOH-extractable pigments orange (7), ascospores averaging less than 15 µm long
4,5$ Ascal apical ring present, amyloid; widespread
4,0$ Ascal apical ring absent; monotypic, not recorded since 1867, ascospores 9-11 × 4.5-5.5 $ H. commutatum Nitschke
5,0$ Mainly on Fagus; ascospores 11-13.5 × 5-6.5 µm $ H. fragiforme (Pers.: Fr.) J. Kickx
5,0$ On other hosts, rarely on Fagus; ascospores 7-9 × 3.5-5 µm $ H. howeanum Peck
6,7$ Stromatal surface with Purple (35) or Vinaceous (57) colors
6,17$ Stromatal surface with Orange (7), Rust (39), Brick (60), or brown colours
6,0$ Stromatal surface with a greenish tone, Isabelline (65), without visible colored granules beneath surface but with Fawn (87) to dilute Umber KOH-extractable pigments; ascospores 11-12.5(-13.5) × 6-6.5 µm, ellipsoidequilateral with straight germ slit, perispore indehiscent in 10% KOH (M.S. & J.F.; unpublished data on specimen in K collected in Poland; identified by Z. Pouzar $ H. papillatum Ellis & Everh.
7,8$ Ascospores averaging more than 20 µm long
7,9$ Ascospores averaging less than 15 µm long
8,0$ KOH-extractable pigments Pale Vinaceous Grey (115) to Vinaceous Grey (116) in fresh specimens or absent in aged specimens; ascospores 18.5-23 × 8-10 µm with germ slit spore- $ H. vogesiacum (Pers.) Sacc.
8,0$ Boreal distribution, frequently on Salix, KOH-extractable pigments dense, Greenish Olivaceous (90); ascospores 22-31 × 8.5-11 µm with faint germ slit less than spore-length $ H. macrosporum P. Karst.
9,10$ KOH-extractable pigments Livid Purple (81) or absent
9,11$ KOH-extractable pigments pigments Orange (7) to Sienna (8)
9,13$ KOH-extractable pigments Amber (47), Isabelline (65), Olivaceous (48), Gray Olivaceous (107), Greenish Olivaceous (90), Citrine (13) or otherwise with yellow, green or brown tones
10,0$ Stromata effused, 0.4-0.5 mm thick, with KOH-extractable pigments dilute, Livid Purple (81) or absent; ascospores 8-11.5 × 4.5-5 µm with a straight germ slit. Distribution world-wide; major stromatal metabolites: carneic acids (Quang et al. 2006) $ H. carneum Petch
10,0$ Stromata pulvinate, 0.8-1.4 mm thick, with KOH-extractable pigments Vinaceous Purple (101); ascospores 9.5-12.5 × 5-6 µm with a sigmoid germ slit (France, UK, present study) $ H. fuscoides J. Fourn., P. Leroy, M. Stadler & Roy Anderson
11,0$ Ascospores 11-13.5 × 5-7 µm, with perispore faintly striate by LM; known from Portugal (present study) $ H. lusitanicum J. Fourn., M. Stadler & Priou
11,12$ Ascospores 9.5-11.5 × 4.5-6 µm, with perispore smooth by LM
12,0$ Stromata widely effused with jagged black margins; ascospores 9-11.5 × 5-6 µm; host preference for Fraxinus; Temperate Europe and USA (Stadler et al. 2008) $ H. petriniae M. Stadler & J. Fourn.
12,0$ Stromata less widely effused to effused-pulvinate, with concolorous margins; ascospores 9.5-11.5 × 4.5-5 µm; known from the Canary Islands (Stadler et al. 2008) $ H. canariense J. Fourn. et al.
13,0$ Perithecia obovoid to frequently tubular, up to 1 mm high; stromatal surface with a metallic shine when mature. Recorded from Central and Western Europe and North America (various hosts). Ascospores 9.5-11.5 × 4-4.8 µm $ H. macrocarpum Pouzar
13,14$ Perithecia spherical to obovoid, not tubular; stromatal surface lacking a metallic shine when mature
14,15$ Ascospores with straight germ slit
14,16$ Ascospores with slightly sigmoid germ slit
15,0$ Ascospores ellipsoid-inequilateral in lateral view, 9-12 × 4-6 µm, perispore dehiscent in 10% KOH. KOH-extractable pigments Amber (47), Greenish Yellow (16) or Citrine (13) $ H. perforatum (Schwein.) Fr.
15,0$ Ascospores ellipsoid, nearly equilateral in lateral view, often pyriform, 12-15 × 5.5-7 µm, perispore indehiscent in 10% KOH. KOH-extractable pigments Olivaceous (48), Greenish Olivaceous (90), Gray olivaceous (127), or Olivaceous Gray (121). So far known from Austria, Germany, Slovakia, and North America $ H. fuscopurpureum (Schwein.) M.A. Curtis
16,0$ Stromata with pure yellow (14) to luteous (12) granules and greenish olivaceous (90) KOH-extractable pigments; apparently restricted to Quercus, with a boreal distribution; ascospores 10-13.5 × 4-5 µm. So far known from France (Stadler et al. 2004), Scandinavia (Granmo 1999) and USA (Stadler et al. 2008 $ H. porphyreum Granmo
16,0$ Stromata with sienna (8) or otherwise orange brown granules and KOHextractable pigments amber (47), isabelline (65), olivaceous (48), gray olivaceous (107), or greenish olivaceous (90); widespread, preferably on Betulaceae and other hosts, but not yet safely recorded from Quercus; ascospores 11-16 × 5-8 µm $ H. fuscum (Pers.) Fr.
17,18$ Young stromata with a bright yellow to orange fimbriate margin; perithecia small, 0.1-0.3 mm diam, seated on a well developed black basal tissue
17,19$ Young stromata lacking a bright yellow to orange fimbriate margin
18,0$ Ascospores 5-7 × 2.5-3.5 µm, ellipsoid-inequilateral in lateral view. So far recorded from Austria, Croatia, Germany (Bitzer et al. 2008). France, Italy, Slovakia (Ripková & Hagara 2003) and Switzerland $ H. ticinense L.E. Petrini 
18,0$ Ascospores 8-11 × 4-5 µm, ellipsoid-equilateral in lateral view. So far recorded from France and USA $ H. subticinense Y.M. Ju & J.D. Rogers
19,20$ KOH-extractable pigments yellow or orange
19,33$ KOH-extractable pigments with shades of olivaceous brown
20,21$ Ascal apical ring highly reduced or lacking, inamyloid
20,23$ Ascal apical ring present, amyloid
21,0$ Stromata glomerate with ostioles encircled by a ring; ascospores 11.5-13 × 6-6.8 µm with perispore conspicuously striate by LM (France, present study $ H. gibriacense J. Fourn., M. Stadler & Gardiennet
21,22$ Stromata applanate to pulvinate, with ostioles lacking a ring; ascospores with perispore smooth by LM
22,0$ Stromata discoid, encircled with a swollen stellate margin, on bark of Fraxinus; ascospores 9.5-12 × 5-6 µm. Europe and North America $ H. cercidicola (Berk. & M.A. Curtis ex Peck) Y.M. Ju & J.D. Rogers
22,0$ Stromata pulvinate to hemispherical, reported from Carpinus, ascospores 9-11 × 4.5-5.5 µm (see also 4) $ H. commutatum Nitschke
23,24$ Ascospores averaging more than 14 µm long
23,25$ Ascospores averaging less than 12 µm long
24,0$ Stromata pulvinate restricted at base, with rust brown to ochraceous brown granules beneath surface; apparently rare, known only from Tilia and Sorbus; ascospores 14-17 × 6.5-8 µm, Recorded from Switzerland (Petrini & Müller 1986), Slovakia and Canada (Stadler et al. 2008) $ H. ferrugineum G. H. Otth
24,0$ Stromata effused to pulvinate, with blood red granules beneath surface, recorded from various hosts; ascospores 15-18 × 6-7.5 µm $ H. julianii L.E. Petrini
25,0$ KOH-extractable pigments Amber (47), Greenish Yellow (16) or Citrine (13); ascospores 9.7-11.5 × 4.7-5.3 µm (see also 15) $ H. perforatum (Schwein.) Fr.
25,26$ KOH-extractable pigments Orange (7), Sienna (8), Rust (39) or Scarlet (5)
26,27$ Ascospores almost equilateral in lateral view
26,28$ Ascospores inequilateral in lateral view
27,0$ Ascospores 7-10 × 3-4.5 µm; stromatal surface dark rust (39) to sepia (63), with dark orange granules beneath surface and KOH-extractable pigments fulvous (43) to rust (39); recorded from Salix in Northern Europe (Granmo 1999), Belgium (J.F. & M.S., unpublished data), and USA (Ju & Rogers 1996, as unnamed segregate in "Notes to H. rubiginosum") $ H. salicicola Granmo
27,0$ Ascospores 9.5-12.5 × 4.8-6 µm; stromatal surface vinaceous buff (86), greyish sepia (106) to brown vinaceous (84) with bright yellow granules beneath surface and between perithecia; KOH-extractable pigments hazel (88), sienna (8) to umber (9). Apparently restricted to Sorbus, with a boreal distribution (Granmo 2001) $ H. liviae Granmo
28,29$ Ascospores averaging less than 11 µm long
28,31$ Ascospores averaging more than 11 µm long
29,30$ Stromata with papillate ostioles
29,0$ Stromata with umbilicate ostioles $ H. rubiginosum (Pers.) Fr.
30,0$ Stromata erumpent, pulvinate, small, with orange granules beneath surface; known from Europe and USA, restricted to Populus; ascospores 8-10 × 3.5-4.5 µm $ H. laschii Nitschke
30,0$ Stromata superficial, effused to pulvinate, with blood red granules beneath surface; distribution apparently world-wide, without apparent host specificity; ascospores 7.5-10 × 4-4.8 µm $ H. rutilum Tul. & C. Tul.
31,0$ Perithecia up to 0.2 mm diam; ascospores 11-14.5 × 5-6 µm with slightly sigmoid germ slit; Canary Islands $ H. urriesii J. Fourn. & M. Stadler
31,32$ Perithecia 0.3-0.45 mm diam; ascospores with straight germ slit
32,0$ Ascospores 9.5-11.5 × 4.5-5 µm; known from the Canary Islands (see also 12) $ H. canariense J. Fourn. et al.
32,0$ Ascospores 11-13.5 × 5-7 µm; known from Portugal (see also 11) $ H. lusitanicum J. Fourn., M. Stadler & Priou
33,0$ KOH-extractable pigments Hazel (88), Sienna (8) to Umber (9); perithecia obovoid, up to 0.4 mm high; ascospores dark brown, ellipsoid, nearly equilateral, 9.5-12.5 × 4.8-6 µm; apparently restricted to Sorbus (see also 27) $ H. liviae Granmo
33,0$ KOH-extractable pigments isabelline (65), umber (9), or grayish sepia (106); perithecia frequently tubular, up to 1 mm high; ascospores inequilateral and narrower, 9.5-11.5 × 4-4.8 µm (see also 13) $ H. macrocarpum Pouzar